[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The inter-AS BGP peering (router1 AS1 ↔ router2 AS2 over 193.10.11.0/24) is healthy now and the partner prefix is stable. Evidence:
- Both peers are Established and exchanging 1 IPv4 prefix each (show ip bgp summary on router1/router2).
- The inter-AS routes are installed and active (router1: B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2: B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1).
- End-to-end reachability pc1↔pc2 is 0% loss with ~1 ms RTT.
- Physical links are steady (eth0/eth1 carrier_changes = 2, carrier=1 on both routers).

What likely looked like “route churn” was a single BGP session reset during daemon startup: at 03:23:39 router1’s bgpd logged “bgp_read_packet error: Connection reset by peer” from 193.10.11.2 (router2). Immediately after, both sides exchanged updates and End-of-RIB, and the session has remained up since (no further drops; keepalives normal). Interfaces show no flapping.

Localization: FRR bgpd control plane between router1 and router2, TCP 179 on router2’s eth0 initiated a one-time reset seen by router1.

Root cause: A transient bgpd restart/initialization on router2 caused a single TCP reset of the BGP session, briefly withdrawing and then re-advertising the partner prefix.

Corrective fix: No action is required; the session is stable. If the routing team still wants to “clear” it, do it on router2 (the side that previously reset the TCP session): either clear the neighbor or restart FRR/bgpd on router2 to reinitialize cleanly.
=== END ===